<section>

<h2>The foundation: Archimedes Principle</h2>

<p>"Eureka!", was surely what the reearchers Aberman et. al thought, when they had the novel idea of employing fluid displacements as a 3D shape sensor. 
</p> 

<p>Archemides' principle (c.246 BC) states:</p>
<quote>Any object, totally or partially immersed in a fluid or liquid, is buoyed up by a force equal to the weight of the fluid displaced by the object.</quote>

<p>i.e the volume of fluid displaced is equal to the volume of the object submerged.</p>


delta V_water = delta V_object submegered



This is the foundation for the logic behind the dip machine. By measuring the level of water displaced as an object is dipped in various orientations, 
we can obtain the 3D dip transform of an object. 

<h3>What is a 3D dip transform?</h3>

Given an object, we can represent it as volumetric binary implicit function:
F(x) = 1 x in the objects interior domain, 0 otherwise

Then the dip transform of the object is given by a time t and orientation theta, wjere the step function acts as a binary answer to the question "is underwater?". 
The time derivative, the rate at which the objects accumulated volume changes over the course of a dip, is equivalent to the Radon trasform of a binary object. The intergral of this is equivalent to exactly the object volume.


The way in which the volume is accumulated, i.e the rate at which the water rises for different thetea, can be used to uniquely define an object shape.

